 "findings":[
  {"heading":"### `format()`","segment_index":"S21.P1","kind":"untrue","en_span":"chapters/dates.qmd:500-500","tr_span":"chapters/dates.pt.qmd:481-481","proposition":"The strptime code %Y produces a 7-digit year. The English states %Y is a 4-digit year (e.g. 1989).","reader_consequence":"A Portuguese reader learns that %Y yields 7 digits, which is impossible for a year, and cannot reconcile it with the accompanying example 1989; the reader may pick a different code when a 4-digit year is required.","confidence":"high"},
  {"heading":"### `format()`","segment_index":"S21.P1","kind":"untrue","en_span":"chapters/dates.qmd:489-489","tr_span":"chapters/dates.pt.qmd:470-470","proposition":"The 001-366 day-of-year number is a 'July day' (Julho). The English says it is the Julian day.","reader_consequence":"A Portuguese reader is told the day-of-year numbering relates to the month of July, so the meaning of %j is lost; the same reader saw the correct term 'dia Juliano' in the earlier list in the same chapter and now has two conflicting definitions.","confidence":"high"},
  {"heading":"## Date display","segment_index":"S20.P1","kind":"untrue","en_span":"chapters/dates.qmd:480-480","tr_span":"chapters/dates.pt.qmd:461-461","proposition":"The undesired default date display that motivates reformatting is '05/01/2018' (DD/MM/YYYY). The English gives '2018-01-05' (the R default YYYY-MM-DD).","reader_consequence":"A Portuguese reader is shown a DD/MM/AAAA string as R's starting display, contradicting the chapter's repeated statement that Date values display as AAAA-MM-DD, and may conclude no reformatting is needed to get an ISO date.","confidence":"high"},
  {"heading":"### **base** R","segment_index":"S7.P2","kind":"untrue","en_span":"chapters/dates.qmd:133-133","tr_span":"chapters/dates.pt.qmd:127-127","proposition":"The strptime abbreviation for minutes is %M (uppercase). The English list gives %m for minutes.","reader_consequence":"The Portuguese and English lists give different codes for minutes, so a reader cross-checking the two handbooks gets contradictory guidance on which abbreviation to pass to format = .","confidence":"medium"},
  {"heading":"### `format()`","segment_index":"S21.P1","kind":"untrue","en_span":"chapters/dates.qmd:502-502","tr_span":"chapters/dates.pt.qmd:483-483","proposition":"The strptime abbreviation for minutes is %M (uppercase). The English list gives %m for minutes.","reader_consequence":"The Portuguese and English lists give different codes for minutes in the format() section, so a reader cross-checking the two handbooks gets contradictory guidance on which abbreviation to pass to format().","confidence":"medium"},
  {"heading":"## Resources","segment_index":"S29.P1","kind":"untrue","en_span":"chapters/dates.qmd:713-714","tr_span":"chapters/dates.pt.qmd:681-681","proposition":"The statmethods 'Online tutorial' and the r-bloggers 'Date formats' post are one resource, joined as 'Tutorial Online de Formatos de datas'. The English lists them as two independent resources on separate lines.","reader_consequence":"A Portuguese reader reads one entry where the English has two, and is told the statmethods tutorial is about the r-bloggers date-formats material, so the independent scope of each link is lost.","confidence":"low"}
 ]}
